From 4b95bc7aa9398f6ff91cd26befd513bfc527e63c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 04:14:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] v4l2codecs: Restrict sink template caps to the device
 frame sizes

The sink pad templates were static and carried no size limits, while
every per-device element is registered with the same rank. On SoCs that
expose more than one stateless decoder for the same codec with different
size limits, autopluggers could pick a decoder that cannot handle the
stream resolution. On RK3588, for example, both the Hantro based
VPU121 (up to 1920x1088) and rkvdec (VDPU381) decode H.264. Whichever
is enumerated first gets the v4l2slh264dec name, and selecting the
Hantro core for a 4K stream fails in gst_v4l2_decoder_set_sink_fmt() with a
negotiation error instead of falling back to the rkvdec element.

Query VIDIOC_ENUM_FRAMESIZES on the coded format at registration and use
the result as width and height ranges in the per-device sink pad
template, the same way the src template is already built per device.
Factory caps then exclude decoders that are too small for the stream,
and the accept-caps query rejects out of range sizes before the format
is set. If the driver does not implement frame size enumeration, the
unrestricted caps are kept.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
